[[build-voidstar]]
=== Voidstar format and schema strings

[abstract]
*Voidstar* is the binary layout every value takes when it leaves a language:
C-like fixed-width slots, with variable-length parts reached through
relative pointers. A *schema string* is a compact description of a type's
layout. A buffer plus its schema is enough for any language to read the
value, with no other type information. Every binding reads and writes this
one layout, so a value crosses between any two languages with no
intermediate format.

==== Schema strings

A schema is a string of type codes in prefix order. Codes that take children
are followed by them; codes that take a count are followed by it.

[cols="2,2,3", options="header"]
|===
| Code | Type | Followed by
| `z` | Unit | nothing
| `b` | Bool | nothing
| `i1` `i2` `i4` `i8` | Signed integer of 1, 2, 4, 8 bytes | -
| `u1` `u2` `u4` `u8` | Unsigned integer | -
| `f4` `f8` | Float (`Real` is `f8`) | -
| `j` | `Int`, arbitrary precision | nothing
| `s` | String (UTF-8) | nothing
| `a` | Array | the element schema; `a:N` declares the length as decimal _N_
| `t` | Tuple | count, then each element schema
| `m` | Record | count, then for each field its name and schema
| `?` | Optional | the inner schema
| `e` | Enum (no constructor has fields) | count, then constructor names
| `v` | Variant (sum type) | count, then for each arm its name, field count and field schemas
| `T` | Table (<<build-tables>>) | optionally `:` count, then column names and schemas
| `F` `O` `I` | Input file, output stream, input stream (<<build-streaming>>) | nothing
| `&` | Names the schema that follows, for recursion | name, then the schema
| `^` | Refers back to an enclosing `&` | name
|===

Counts and name lengths are base-64 numbers. A value below 64 is one digit
from `0-9`, `a-z`, `A-Z`, `+`, `/` (0 to 63). A larger value is written as
`=`, its low digit, and the rest of the number, recursively: 100 is `=A1`.
Names are written as their length followed by their bytes. Enums and variants
have at most 256 constructors, and nesting is limited to 128 levels.

A schema may also carry *hints* in angle brackets (`<dict>`) naming the
concrete type a language should build. Hints do not change the layout and are
dropped from the canonical form stored in packets.

Examples, from real programs:

[cols="2,3", options="header"]
|===
| Schema | Type
| `j` | `Int`
| `v23Err1s2Ok1z` | `Try Str ()`: arm `Err` with one `s` field, arm `Ok` with one `z` field
| `m24names2idi4` | `{name :: Str, id :: I32}`
| `&4Treev24Leaf04Node2^4Tree^4Tree` | `data Tree = Leaf \| Node Tree Tree`
| `t3sjaau1` | A function value: home language, function index, captured values as packets
|===

==== Layout

Every value has a fixed-width *slot*. Integers are little-endian.

[cols="1,1,1,4", options="header"]
|===
| Code | Width | Align | Slot contents
| `z` | 0 | 1 | Nothing.
| `b`, `i1`, `u1` | 1 | 1 | The value.
| `i2` ... `f8` | 2, 4, 8 | natural | The value.
| `e` | 1 | 1 | Constructor ordinal, in declaration order.
| `s`, `a` | 16 | 8 | 8-byte count (bytes for a string, with no NUL; elements for an array), then a relative pointer to the data. An empty one holds the null pointer.
| `j` | 16 | 8 | 8-byte limb count, then a word. With one limb the word is the value itself. With more, it points to that many 64-bit limbs, least significant first, in two's complement.
| `t`, `m` | computed | `t`: largest field; `m`: 8 | C struct rules: each field at the next offset aligned for it, total rounded up to the largest field alignment. Field names exist only in the schema.
| `?` | 8 | 8 | A relative pointer to the value, or null when absent.
| `v` | 16 | 8 | Byte 0 is the arm's ordinal, bytes 1-7 zero, bytes 8-15 a pointer to the arm's fields laid out as a tuple. An arm with no fields stores null.
|===

Array elements are packed at the element's width. The data of an array of
numbers starts on a 64-byte boundary, so a binding can hand it to vector
instructions or a numeric library without copying. A record `{name :: Str,
age :: I32}` puts `name` at offset 0 and `age` at 16, with width 24.

Variant and optional slots hold a pointer rather than the value itself so
that every slot's width is fixed. This is what lets a recursive type exist: a
`Tree` slot is 16 bytes however deep the tree goes. For the same reason, a
type may refer to itself only through an array, optional or variant.

**Relative pointers.** A pointer is a signed 64-bit integer. Bits 62-48 name a
shared-memory volume and bits 47-0 an offset into it (<<build-shm>>); -1 is
null. Because a pointer names a volume rather than an address, it means the
same thing in every process that maps the volumes. When a value is copied out
of shared memory into one self-contained buffer (to send inline or write to
disk), its pointers use volume 0 and are offsets from the start of the
buffer; loading the buffer back into shared memory rebases them.

**Reading a value** takes only the schema and the buffer: the schema gives
each slot's width and each field's offset, a pointer is followed to reach
variable-length data, and a variant's tag selects which arm's schema governs
its payload.

**Other encodings.** A value can also be written as JSON or MessagePack, for
the command line, files and caches. MessagePack writes tuples and records as
arrays, enums as ordinals, and variants as `[tag, payload]`; JSON writes
records as objects and constructors by name. Tables have neither form and
travel only as Arrow.

